(project pricklypear-lisp-interpreter
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: lisp-interpreter")
 (status implemented)
 (* agent note (|2026-08-02 status reconcile: eval TCO+budget + macros (defmacro/qq/expand) shipped; dune runtest lisp 123 OK; define/defmacro persist synchronous.|) *)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section lisp-interpreter
  (doc "Pure interpreter for Nopales (a Scheme-flavored Lisp-1), AST-as-source, primitives layer")
  (status implemented)
  
  (subsection language-roadmap
   (doc "Nopales trajectory phases 1–3: code-as-data law, dialect ergonomics, production capabilities")
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (smoke "phase exit criteria listed per phase; phases 1–2 and public-route half of 3 are green")
    (test "conformance suite pins phase 1–2 surface"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         NORTH STAR
           Nopales is a small Scheme-family impure functional language
           for a live multi-user image (humans + agents + Postgres).
           Not pure FP. Not R7RS-complete. Not a static-typed ML.

         STATUS
           Phase 1 EXIT — symbols + errors-and-locations implemented.
           Phase 2 EXIT — binding-sugar, match, std-library, guard.
           Phase 3 PARTIAL EXIT — public Route_gate + package-surface
             + budgets enforced; broader per-agent tool masks remain
             additive follow-on (see capability-enforcement).
           core-freeze contract + conformance suite enforce the seam.
           Phase 4+ (modules-lite / hygiene / maps): still out of scope
           until real dogfood pain.

         PHASE 1 — HONEST CODE-AS-DATA (done)
           symbols; errors-and-locations (kinds + parse loc + guard).
           Exit: 'a vs "a"; symbolic AST heads; structured errors;
           dune runtest lisp green.

         PHASE 2 — PLEASANT DIALECT (done)
           binding-sugar; match; std-library freeze; guard catch.
           Exit: define (f x)…; begin multi-body; match on trees;
           frozen list/string/symbol helpers; guard recovers.

         PHASE 3 — PRODUCTION CAPABILITIES (public gate done)
           effects inference + Route_gate public allowlist;
           package-surface exports; eval budgets hard leash.
           Remaining additive: denser agent-session effect masks.

         DECISION DEFAULTS (locked)
           - Impure functional Scheme-family dialect
           - Dynamic typing + effects + data schemas (not HM types)
           - Unhygienic macros + gensym (no syntax-case in 1–3)
           - No call/cc
           - No in-Nopales fiber/async API (host owns concurrency)
           - Branches remain the override model; modules-lite = P4+
           - JSON/dict prims for records; no VMap required in 1–3
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection core-freeze
   (doc "Nopales core 1.0 freeze: the frozen language contract and the slow-change rule that produces long-term stability")
   (status implemented)
   (implements lisp/test/conformance_test.ml borge/lisp-interpreter.borg)
   (verify
    (smoke "core 1.0 frozen-surface list is enumerated and matches the reader, value, special-form, primitive, effect, and error chapters")
    (smoke "a proposed core change is classifiable as additive, deprecate-then-remove, or break against this rule")
    (test "conformance suite exists, is service-free, and runs green on a clean core"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         THE CONTRACT. This subsection is the slow-change law for
         Nopales core. Phases 1–2 of language-roadmap and the public
         route half of Phase 3 have exited; the frozen-surface list
         below is the working core 1.0 contract enforced by the
         conformance suite. Further additive work is allowed; silent
         meaning changes are not.

         FROZEN SURFACE (core 1.0):
           - Reader surface syntax: s-expressions, quote/quasiquote/
             unquote/unquote-splicing shorthand, parse errors with
             line and column.
           - Value model: VStr, VInt, VFloat, VBool, VSymbol, VPair,
             VList, VLambda, VMacro, VPrim, VRef, VNil, and the JSON
             codec that serializes values/exprs into function_defs.
           - Special forms: quote, quasiquote, unquote,
             unquote-splicing, define (incl. function sugar + doc),
             lambda, if, let, letrec, begin/seq, match, guard, error,
             defmacro, cond, and, or, apply, list/map|filter|foldl|
             foldr, gensym, macroexpand*, with-transaction, and the
             image control forms checkout/fork/merge/route when bound.
             New special forms may be added; existing meanings do not
             change in place.
           - Pure/primitive set: arithmetic, comparison, list, string,
             symbol, predicate, type-of, whoami, current-author,
             dict-*, and the std-library freeze table. Optional args
             with defaults OK; silent return-shape change is a break.
           - Effect lattice: capability tags, infer, policy_allows,
             Route_gate public allowlist. Adding a tag is additive;
             redefining an existing tag's meaning is a core bump.
           - Error kinds + locations (interp_error_kind, Parse_error
             line:col, guard catch surface, budget uncatchable).

         SLOW-CHANGE RULE. A core change is one of:
           - ADDITIVE: new prim / special form / effect tag / optional
             arg with default preserving old behavior. Always allowed.
           - DEPRECATE-THEN-REMOVE: add new name/behavior, keep old
             under deprecation, remove only across a named release.
           - BREAK: only with explicit core version bump (Nopales 2.0)
             and a migration path for product code.

         PRODUCT LAYER IS NOT FROZEN. libs/*, user branches, routes,
         themes change freely via define/route (OCaml slow, Nopales
         fast). The freeze is the seam that makes the fast layer safe.

         ENFORCEMENT. lisp/test/conformance_test.ml — real Nopales
         programs with expected values, service-free, in dune runtest.
         A reddened case does not land until the suite is updated
         intentionally and the change is classified under this rule.
         borge drift guards structure; conformance guards semantics.

         VERSIONING. Nopales core versioned separately from the
         Pricklypear framework; coupled only at a major core bump.

         FOLLOW-ONS (additive, not freeze blockers):
           - Broader per-agent effect_mask on every tool prim
           - symbols phase C (strict string-eq)
           - denser product-package pure snippets in conformance
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection conformance
   (doc "Conformance suite: real Nopales programs with expected eval results, run on every core change as the semantic gate of the freeze")
   (status implemented)
   (implements lisp/test/conformance_test.ml lisp/test/dune lisp/conformance/conformance.ml)
   (verify
    (test "suite loads and evaluates each program against its expected value with no services")
    (test "a deliberate core change that alters a program's result is caught as a failure")
    (build "dune runtest lisp includes conformance"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         WHAT IT IS. A set of real Nopales programs, each paired
         with the value it must evaluate to, run by the pure
         interpreter with no services. Tier between OCaml unit
         tests and HTTP smokes: the LANGUAGE gate of core-freeze.

         LOCATION. lisp/test/conformance_test.ml — alcotest cases
         of the form (name, program, expected-pp-value) plus a few
         multi-form setup programs. Runs under dune runtest lisp.

         CONTENTS (v1 frozen-surface cases):
           - reader: quote, quasiquote/unquote, lists, strings, bools,
             string escapes (named, \\ddd byte, \\uXXXX unicode scalar)
           - values: VInt VStr VSymbol VBool VNil VList distinctness
           - special forms: define sugar, lambda, let, if, begin,
             match, guard, cond, and/or, defmacro/gensym
           - pure list HOF: list/map list/filter list/foldl list/foldr
           - errors: unbound kind; guard recovers; budget uncatchable
           - symbols: 'a vs "a"; car of quoted lambda is symbol
         Product-package headline cases that need DB/HTTP stay in
         the smoke tier (libs/verify). Pure kernels of those
         packages may be added here as pure snippets over time.

         RUN MODEL. Service-free pure interpreter only. A reddened
         case blocks core change land until the suite is updated
         intentionally and reclassified (additive / deprecate /
         break per core-freeze).

         SCOPE. Pure eval results only. DB prims and routes → smoke.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection ast
   (doc "The expr and value types")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         type expr =
           | Var of string
           | Lit of value
           | App of expr * expr list
           | Let of string * expr * expr
           | Letrec of (string * expr) list * expr
           | If of expr * expr * expr
           | Lambda of string list * expr
           | Prim of string * expr list
           | Seq of expr * expr
           | Quote of expr                 (* data quote; see macros *)
           | Quasiquote of expr            (* macros.quasiquote *)
           | Unquote of expr
           | UnquoteSplicing of expr

         type value =
           | VInt of int64
           | VFloat of float
           | VStr of string
           | VSymbol of string               (* see subsection symbols *)
           | VBool of bool
           | VNil
           | VPair of value * value           (* cons cells *)
           | VLambda of string list * expr * env
           | VMacro of string list * expr * env
               (* defmacro transformer; params bind to UNEVALUATED
                  arg exprs reified as values via quote_to_value;
                  body returns a value tree that value_to_expr
                  turns back into an expr for re-eval. See macros. *)
           | VPrim of string                  (* primitive name *)
           | VRef of type_name * id           (* tagged ref into store *)
           | VList of value list              (* sugar for nested pairs *)

         The AST IS the source (homoiconicity). Stored as
         JSONB in function_defs.body. The reader (s-expr
         parser) produces expr values; the printer
         round-trips them. No parser/printer asymmetry.

         Closures capture their defining env (lexical
         scope). VLambda carries env. No dynamic scope.

         VRef carries its target type so the interpreter
         can verify derefs at call time before querying
         the store.

         VALUE ATOMS vs CODE NAMES:
           At the expr layer, Var s and Lit (VStr s) are distinct.
           At the value layer, VSymbol holds code names and VStr
           holds string data (see subsection symbols). Quote /
           read-string / function-ast trees match Scheme-family
           code-as-data.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection ast-diff
   (doc "Pure structural diff over expr trees (consumer: branching.diff). Not the surface prim — the pure walker.")
   (status implemented)
   (implements lisp/lib/ast_diff.ml lisp/test/ast_diff_test.ml)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-16 ralph loop 19: implemented + verified (lisp/lib/ast_diff.ml, 27/27 ast_diff tests green, full runtest exit 0). Pure module, no I/O, tests with no services (dune runtest lisp). Depends on the frozen expr type (core-freeze) and analyze.ast_hash for the equality fast-path. Semantics authority: branching.diff — this subsection is the module home, not the semantics.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build lisp")
    (test "lisp/test/ast_diff_test.ml — same/modified/replaced over all 11 expr constructors")
    (test "expr_to_json round-trip preserved: diff never mutates, only reads"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         Pure walker over the expr type (see ast). No I/O,
         no Postgres — tests with dune runtest lisp alone,
         like reader / eval / env.

         type delta =
           | Same
           | Replaced of { from: expr; to_: expr }
           | Node   of { ctor: string;
                       children: (string * delta) list }

         val diff : Ast.expr -> Ast.expr -> delta

         Per-ctor recursion (fixed-arity positional for
         If/Seq/Lambda/Let/Define/Quote; list-LCS for
         App.args and Letrec.bindings) and non-goals
         (rename detection, alpha-equivalence, param
         order) are spec'd in branching.diff — this
         subsection is the module home, not the
         semantics authority.

         STAKEHOLDER on core-freeze: ast_diff is a
         structural consumer of every expr constructor.
         Adding a constructor to expr requires a diff
         case here (exhaustive match) — borge drift will
         reddens a missed case, but the freeze contract
         (see core-freeze) naming a reason the expr type
         is slow-change now includes this module.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection symbols
   (doc "Distinguish symbols from strings at the value layer (undo v1 collapse)")
   (status implemented)
   (implements lisp/lib/ast.ml lisp/lib/eval.ml lisp/lib/reader.ml pure/prims_pure.ml image/lib/primitives.ml)
   (verify
    (test "quote hello is symbol; quote \"hello\" is string; not equal")
    (test "value_to_expr round-trip preserves Var vs Lit VStr without __str__")
    (test "gensym returns VSymbol")
    (test "symbol? string? symbol=? symbol->string string->symbol")
    (test "type-of distinguishes symbol and str")
    (smoke "function-ast / read-string heads are symbols; ui/home still renders"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         SHIPPED (phases A + B). Code-as-data trees use VSymbol for
         names; strings stay VStr. Collapse is REJECTED.

         VALUES
           (quote hello)   => VSymbol "hello"
           (quote "hello") => VStr "hello"
           (symbol? 'lambda) => #t
           (string? "lambda") => #t
           (symbol=? 'a 'a) => #t
           (car (read-string "(lambda (x) x)")) => symbol lambda

         TYPE + PRINT/READ
           OCaml: | VSymbol of string
           JSON values: {"sym":"hello"} vs {"str":"hello"}
           expr JSONB unchanged ({var}/{str} already distinct) —
           no migration of function_defs bodies.
           Printer: VSymbol bare; VStr double-quoted.
           Reader: bare token -> Var; "…" -> Lit VStr.

         BRIDGE
           quote_to_value: Var -> VSymbol; Lit VStr stays VStr;
             App heads that were Vars become VSymbol heads in VList.
           value_to_expr: VSymbol -> Var; VStr -> Lit VStr (never Var).
           reify_expr / macros use quote_to_value (no __str__ primary).
           gensym returns VSymbol (optional string prefix).

         PRIMITIVES (image prims_pure; registered at boot)
           symbol? string? symbol=? symbol->string string->symbol
           type-of -> "symbol" | "str" | …
           as-str ACCEPTS VSymbol (HTML/path ergonomics)
           string-eq: phase-B compat still coerces via as-str/coerce_str
             so product packages do not mass-break. Prefer symbol=?
             for AST car walks. Strict string-eq (reject symbols) is
             deferred cleanup (phase C), not required for this section.

         DEFERRED (phase C cleanup — non-blocking)
           - Remove residual __str__ unwrap paths once no inflight macros
           - Strict string-eq (VStr only)
           - value_to_expr: refuse VStr heads without string->symbol

         NON-GOALS
           - Full interning / eq identity across processes
           - Keyword type (:foo)
           - Changing expr JSONB shape

         SUCCESS
           'a and "a" are different; code-as-data heads are symbols;
           macros need no __str__; symbol prims exist; product home
           and pure lisp tests green. No DB migration.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection errors-and-locations
   (doc "Structured interpreter errors with source locations; catch surface for packages")
   (status implemented)
   (implements lisp/lib/ast.ml lisp/lib/eval.ml lisp/lib/reader.ml image/lib/http_pages.ml image/lib/agent_tools.ml)
   (verify
    (test "parse error includes line:col")
    (test "unbound var error includes name and optional span")
    (test "guard/with-handlers catches User_error and continues")
    (test "guard does not catch Budget_exceeded")
    (smoke "/api/eval error JSON includes kind and loc fields when available"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         SHIPPED SURFACE (v1)

         Host signal remains OCaml Interp_error. Every common failure
         has a stable kind via interp_error_kind (snake_case):
           unbound_var | not_a_function | arity_mismatch |
           wrong_arg_count | type_mismatch | budget_exceeded |
           prim_error | parse_error | user_error

         type interp_error =
           | Unbound_var of string
           | Not_a_function of value
           | Arity_mismatch of string * int * int
           | Wrong_arg_count of string * int * int
           | Type_mismatch of string * value
           | Budget_exceeded
           | Prim_error of string * string
           | Parse_error of string * int * int  (* msg, line, col *)
           | User_error of string

         Locations:
           - Reader_error / Parse_error carry line:col (reader state).
           - Unbound_var carries the name; optional span on every expr
             is deferred (not required for v1 catch/API).
           - interp_error_loc returns Some (line,col) for Parse_error
             and None otherwise.

         /api/eval error JSON (additive fields; .error always present):
           { ok: false, error: "…", kind: "unbound_var",
             name: "foo",            ; when Unbound_var
             line: 12, col: 4 }      ; when loc known
         Old clients that only read .error keep working.

         CATCH SURFACE — special form guard (not with-handlers):
           (guard handler-lambda body…)
           Handler is a 0- or 1-arg lambda. On Interp_error (except
           Budget_exceeded, which always re-raises), the 1-arg form
           receives a tagged list:
             (error <message-str> <kind-symbol>)
           Nested guard is OK. Handler return value becomes the
           result of the guard form.

         (error msg) | (error kind msg):
           raises User_error. Kind+msg concatenates as "kind: msg"
           in the message string; structured kind on the OCaml side
           remains user_error (v1).

         RELATED — image.dnu-stubs (product holes, not catch)
           doesNotUnderstand-style stub+callers UX reuses unbound_var
           kind/name fields from this JSON but does not replace guard.

         DEFERRED (not blocking implemented)
           - Full loc = {source; line; col} on every raise site
           - Optional span field on expr AST
           - Stack traces as first-class lists
           - CL-style restarts / with-handlers multi-pred dispatch

         NON-GOALS
           - Catching host bugs / OCaml exceptions unrelated to eval
           - Full CL condition system

         SUCCESS
           Common eval failures expose kind + message; parse/read
           paths carry line:col; packages can guard fallible work
           and render a friendly value without killing the page;
           budgets remain an uncatchable hard leash by default.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection reader
   (doc "The reader turns source text into AST; the AST is the source of truth (homoiconic, persisted as JSONB); read/print roundtrip fidelity is what makes stored code trustworthy.")
   (status implemented)
   (implements lisp/lib/reader.ml lisp/test/conformance_test.ml)
   (verify
    (build "dune build lisp")
    (test "named + \\ddd + \\uXXXX escapes decode; \\uXXXX UTF-8-encodes to 1–4 bytes")
    (test "\"\\u203e\" reads to the single-char overline ‾, not literal u203e")
    (test "lone surrogate \\uD800..\\uDFFF raises Reader_error")
    (test "read/print roundtrip of a string holding ‾ preserves it (escape_string emits \\ddd bytes)")
    (test "forgiving fallback: unknown \\x still yields literal x")
    (test "conformance pins \\uXXXX decode so silent removal reddens the suite"))

   (details
    (doc (|
         The reader parses Nopales surface syntax to expr
         values. Standard s-expressions: (foo bar baz),
         'symbol, "string", 123, #t, #f, ().

         Surface syntax is just an editor concern: a
         function stored in Postgres is its AST (JSONB),
         and the s-expr syntax is one of several possible
         editors (along with the htmx tree-editor that
         edits the AST directly).

         Special forms recognized at read or eval time
         (decision: eval time, the reader is generic):
           (lambda (args) body)
           (let ((name val)...) body)
           (letrec ((name val)...) body)
           (if test then else)
           (define name expr)        (* top-level binding *)
           (quote x)
           (quasiquote x)            (* macros; sugar `x *)
           (unquote x)               (* macros; sugar ,x *)
           (unquote-splicing x)      (* macros; sugar ,@x *)
           (defmacro name (params) body)  (* macros *)
           (deftype name (fields ...))
           (fork parent child)
           (merge source target)
           (app "prim-name" args)    (* explicit prim call *)

         Reader sugar for macros (in addition to 'x -> (quote x)):
           `x   -> (quasiquote x)
           ,x   -> (unquote x)
           ,@x  -> (unquote-splicing x)
         Comma forms are only meaningful under quasiquote; bare
         unquote at top-level is a reader-or-eval error.

         Comments: ; to end of line.

         STRING ESCAPES. Inside a "..." literal the reader
         recognizes, in order of precedence:
           \n \t \r \\ \" \'        named single-char escapes
           \ddd                     exactly-three-digit DECIMAL byte
                                    (OCaml String.escaped form; the
                                    vault/export path for non-ASCII
                                    and control bytes; n must be ≤255)
           \uXXXX                   exactly-four-hex-digit UNICODE
                                    scalar (see below)
           \x  (any other char)     forgiving: literal x, the
                                    backslash is dropped — editor
                                    over-escapes and paste noise do
                                    not hard-fail

         \uXXXX UNICODE ESCAPE (additive; closes the "u203e" gap).
           Four hex digits (0-9 a-f A-F), case-insensitive. The
           scalar is UTF-8 ENCODED into the byte buffer (1–4 bytes),
           because values/AST serialize to JSONB and CSS/HTML output
           expects UTF-8 text, not a raw low byte.
             - BMP scalar U+0000..U+FFFF except the surrogate range
               D800..DFFF: encode directly.
             - Surrogate escape \uD800..\uDFFF: NOT a valid scalar
               alone → Reader_error "bad unicode escape \uXXXX".
               v1 does not combine surrogate pairs; write the actual
               non-BMP char or use two-step decimal bytes if ever
               needed. This is a deliberate smallness call, not an
               oversight.
           Before this existed, "\u203e" fell through to the
           forgiving rule and became the literal 4 chars "u203e" —
           this was the visible menu-caret bug (libs/ui) and the
           degree-sign bug (libs/weather). Classified ADDITIVE under
           core-freeze: it only gives meaning to a backslash sequence
           that previously produced garbage; no existing valid program
           changes meaning (a prior "\u203e" was already broken).

         Docstrings (see define-docstring): optional metadata on
         a top-level binding. Surface forms the reader already
         produces as plain AST (no dedicated constructor):
           (define name expr "doc")
           (define (name p…) "doc" body…)
           (define name (lambda (p…) "doc" body…))
         The string is not a value binding; eval and the image
         strip it into function_defs.doc (column already on
         storage.schema).
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection eval
   (doc "The evaluator: env * expr -> value")
   (implements lisp/lib/env.ml lisp/lib/eval.ml)
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: trampoline TCO, budget steps, pure eval+Prim_call; lisp tests green. define persist no longer Lwt.async.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build lisp")
    (test "dune runtest — lisp eval + TCO cases")
    (smoke "tail self-recursion depth >> OCaml stack without overflow")
    (smoke "non-tail recursion still correct (e.g. factorial)")
    (smoke "infinite tail loop hits Budget_exceeded, not hang/stack"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         The evaluator is PURE. It takes an env and an expr,
         returns a value or raises an interpreter error.
         No I/O in the interpreter. All side effects go
         through Prim calls, which the IMAGE layer wires to
         actual Postgres/SSH/HTTP side-effects.

         Cooperative evaluation: the eval loop is stepped;
         each Prim invocation yields back to the image's
         event loop. This gives per-session budgeting:
         - A step counter per call (default_limit 100_000)
         - When exceeded, that ONE call aborts
         - The session continues
         - (budget-remaining) reads the active call's leftover
           steps (dynamic current_budget)
         - OCaml prim bodies do not tick; only the call site
         This prevents "user's bad function wedges the
         image" entirely. Scale bugs are fixed by cheaper
         paths (notes.budget-scale), not by raising the limit.

         (define name expr) at top-level adds binding to
         current session's env AND persists to store. Edit
         = redefine (update the row). No versioning at the
         binding level beyond what branches provide.

         Optional docstring (define-docstring): trailing string
         after the value, or leading string in a multi-body
         define/lambda, is accepted by eval (not bound as the
         value) and persisted by the image into function_defs.doc.

         Application: evaluate operator + args, apply.
         Primitives are dispatched by name through a table
         the IMAGE layer registers at boot. The interpreter
         knows nothing about Postgres or htmx.

         Special forms handled in eval, not reader: lambda,
         let, letrec, if, define, quote, quasiquote,
         unquote, unquote-splicing, defmacro, plus higher-order
         list forms list/map, list/filter, list/foldl (OCaml
         loops + apply — not recursive Nopales frames). The reader
         produces App/Variants of these as plain AST (or the
         dedicated Quote/Quasiquote/Unquote constructors when
         sugar is used — either representation is fine as long
         as quote_to_value / expand / pp round-trip).

         Macro expansion is part of eval application (and of
         define/load — see macros.expansion-timing). Eval must
         call expand before ordinary arg evaluation when the
         operator resolves to a VMacro.

         =========================================================
         TAIL-CALL OPTIMIZATION (TCO)
         =========================================================
         Proper Scheme-style TCO via an explicit trampoline in
         eval.ml (OCaml does not guarantee TCO on recursive calls).

         Model:
           eval_step returns Done value | Tail (env, expr)
           eval / eval_force runs a loop: while Tail, continue
           without growing the OCaml stack.

         Tail positions (must bounce, not OCaml-recurse):
           - if/cond: chosen branch
           - and/or: last subform (and short-circuit non-tails force)
           - seq: last form; earlier forms forced
           - let / letrec: body
           - lambda application: body after binding args
             (the App node is always tail in its own frame;
              nested Apps as arguments are forced first)
           - macro expansion result: evaluated in tail position
           - list/map|filter|foldl: stay OCaml loops; closure
             bodies use eval_force (each element is non-tail
             relative to the list form)

         Non-tail positions force to a value (may use OCaml stack
         for the force frame only, not for the tail chain inside).

         Budget: every step still ticks. Infinite tail loops hit
         Budget_exceeded — never hang, never exhaust OS stack.

         Effects: Prim_call still perform; after return, continue
         the trampoline. Rim re-entry (with-transaction,
         apply_lambda) uses the same eval_force entry point.

         Not required: full CPS, custom VM, or rest-args frames.
         Heap env frames still allocate per call (TCO saves stack).
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection macros
   (doc "User-defined syntactic macros: defmacro, quasiquote, value<->expr bridge, expand-at-define")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: VMacro, defmacro, quasiquote/unquote, macroexpand-1/macroexpand, gensym; persist as __macro__ wrapper; gate tests cover macro hiding create.|) *)
   (implements lisp/lib/eval.ml lisp/lib/reader.ml lisp/lib/ast.ml lisp/lib/special_forms.ml)
   (verify
    (build "dune build lisp")
    (test "dune runtest -- lisp  — macro/quasiquote/value_to_expr cases")
    (smoke "(defmacro when (t b) `(if ,t ,b ())) then (when #t 1) => 1")
    (smoke "top-level defmacro persists; reload expands call sites on load"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ==========================================================
         0. WHY MACROS, WHY NOW
         ==========================================================

         Philosophy parked macros ("can come later"). The habitat
         is past that: product packages, AI authoring, and agents
         writing Nopales all want surface sugar without growing the
         OCaml special-form table forever. Homoiconicity is already
         real (AST-as-JSONB); macros are the missing half — code
         that writes code as data, then re-enters eval.

         Goals for v1:
           - Users/agents can define (when / unless / and / or …)
             in Nopales packages without OCaml changes
           - Macro bodies use quasiquote so templates are readable
           - Expansion is inspectable (macroexpand-1)
           - Persisted function bodies remain readable source ASTs
             (macro CALL sites stored; expand on load/define)
           - No hygiene engine; gensym for accidental capture

         Non-goals for v1:
           - Hygienic macros / syntax-case / syntax-rules
           - Reader macros / custom dispatch chars
           - Compiler-style whole-program expand
           - Separate macro package object type (macros are
             ordinary bindings of value VMacro on a branch)
           - Macro-defining macros (allowed if it falls out;
             not a success criterion)
           - Changing the Lwt eval ABI

         ==========================================================
         1. SURFACE SYNTAX
         ==========================================================

         (defmacro name (p1 p2 …) body)
           - Special form (name not evaluated; params not evaluated)
           - Binds name in the CURRENT env to a VMacro
           - Top-level defmacro ALSO persists like define:
             image layer writes function_defs (or equivalent)
             with a kind/tag distinguishing macro from lambda
             so reload reinstalls VMacro, not VLambda.
             v1 acceptable encoding: store body AST as
               (defmacro name (params) body)  OR
               body with metadata flag is_macro=true.
             Prefer a boolean/kind on function_defs if a column
             already exists for kind; else store the defmacro
             wrapper AST so load can detect head=defmacro.
           - Returns the VMacro (or name string) — mirror define's
             "return the bound value" convention unless image
             layer already normalizes define returns.

         (quote x) / 'x
           - Unchanged: expr -> value via quote_to_value

         (quasiquote template) / `template
           - Evaluates template structure, interpolating unquotes
           - Outside any quasiquote, bare unquote is an error

         (unquote x) / ,x
           - Only valid inside quasiquote; evaluates x in the
             current env and splices the resulting VALUE into
             the template as a single element

         (unquote-splicing x) / ,@x
           - Only valid inside quasiquote list position
           - x must evaluate to a VList (or proper list of pairs);
             elements are spliced into the parent list
           - Error if used in non-list / atomic template position

         (macroexpand-1 form)
           - Special form OR pure prim: if form is (name . args)
             and name is bound to VMacro, run ONE expansion step
             and return the expansion as a value tree (quoted
             shape). If not a macro call, return form unchanged
             (as value via quote_to_value).
           - Does not eval the expansion. (Current impl: evals macro body to produce the value tree; matches inbox language-design note c11-4013 contract for REPL inspection.)

         (macroexpand form)
           - Repeat macroexpand-1 until fixed point or budget
             tick limit; return final value tree.

         (gensym) / (gensym prefix)
           - Pure prim: returns a fresh symbol name as VStr
             (e.g. "g$42" or "prefix$42"). Counter is process-
             local (image or lisp package ref cell). Enough for
             unhygienic capture avoidance in v1.

         ==========================================================
         2. VALUE <-> EXPR BRIDGE (the hard kernel)
         ==========================================================

         Already exists:
           quote_to_value : expr -> value
             Lit v -> v
             Var s -> VStr s
             App/special constructors -> nested VList with
               head symbol strings ("if", "lambda", …)

         REQUIRED for macros:
           value_to_expr : value -> expr
             Inverse, best-effort, for trees macros produce.

         Macro parameter contract (updated):
           Macro arguments are bound as self-evaluated literal VALUES
           (plain VStr for strings, VInt/VFloat/etc. for numbers).
           No __str__ tagging at the macro call boundary.
           The __str__ emit workaround is deprecated.

         Rules (v1):
           VInt/VFloat/VBool/VNil/VStr that are self-evaluating
             literals -> Lit v
           VStr s  used in OPERATOR / BINDING / PARAM position
             of a list tree -> Var s
             (i.e. when value_to_expr sees VList (VStr head :: args),
              head becomes Var head unless head is a known self-
              quoting literal form — see below)
           VList [] -> Lit VNil   (* empty list *)
           VList (VStr "quote" :: [x]) -> Quote (value_to_expr x)
             or App(Var "quote", …) — either OK if eval handles both
           VList (h :: t) -> App (value_to_expr h, map value_to_expr t)
           VPair chain treated like list if proper; improper pair
             error or dotted App — v1: require proper lists from
             macros; raise User_error on improper
           VLambda / VMacro / VPrim / VRef as template leaves:
             error (cannot reify live closures into source expr)

         Symbols (Lisp-1 + VSymbol — see subsection symbols):
           (quote hello) => VSymbol "hello"
           (quote "hello") => VStr "hello"
           Macro args use quote_to_value (symbols for Vars, strings
           for Lit VStr). Residual __str__ unwrap is transitional
           only (phase C cleanup).

         Round-trip invariant (tests):
           For any expr e built from reader surface (no live
           closures): value_to_expr (quote_to_value e) is
           alpha-equivalent / pp-equal enough to eval the same
           for pure programs. Exact AST equality preferred where
           constructors align.

         ==========================================================
         3. EXPANSION SEMANTICS
         ==========================================================

         A VMacro is (params, body_expr, defining_env).

         When applying a macro (name a1 a2 … an):
           1. Do NOT eval a1…an.
           2. Reify each ai with quote_to_value -> vi
           3. Bind params to vi in a child of defining_env
              (lexical macro env — captures helpers at defmacro)
           4. Eval body in that env -> expansion_value
           5. expansion_expr = value_to_expr expansion_value
           6. Eval expansion_expr in the ORIGINAL call-site env
              (not the macro's defining env) — standard Nopales
              macro call-site evaluation of the expansion.

         Recursive expansion:
           If expansion_expr is itself a macro call, expand again
           (macroexpand loop) subject to the same Budget.t as
           eval. Infinite macro recursion => Budget_exceeded.

         Shadowing:
           If a local binding shadows a macro name with a non-
           macro value, App uses ordinary application.
           is_special_form still wins over macros for the fixed
           kernel names (lambda, if, quote, defmacro, …).
           defmacro names MUST NOT be allowed to override kernel
           special forms; define/defmacro of those names errors.

         ==========================================================
         4. EXPANSION TIMING (define / load / REPL)
         ==========================================================

         Two moments matter:

         A) Interactive / one-shot eval (REPL, /api/eval):
            Expand at use (call time), as in §3. Simple and
            correct for ad-hoc forms.

         B) Top-level (define name body) and image load of
            function_defs:
            After reading body AST, run a FULL macroexpand on
            the body BEFORE storing the evaluated VLambda in
            the session env. Rationale:
              - Call sites stay fast (already expanded in the
                in-memory lambda body)
              - Effect inference (lisp-interpreter.effects) sees
                real prims, not macro heads
              - AI/tools that read env closures see expanded code

         Persistence choice (IMPORTANT):
           Store the SOURCE body AST (with macro call sites),
           not only the expanded form.
             function_defs.body = source (what user/agent wrote)
             On session install / load-library / define:
               expand(source) -> eval -> VLambda in env
           Why: tree editor, pp, package export (lib.pp), and
           AI rewrite tools must show what the author wrote.
           Expanded-only storage would destroy macro use sites.

           Optional later cache column expanded_body — not v1.

         defmacro persistence:
           On load, re-bind VMacro from stored defmacro AST
           BEFORE expanding other defines that might call it.
           Install order on a branch:
             1. all macros (defmacro forms)
             2. then functions (define forms), each expanded
                against the macro env so far
           Within a single load batch, dependency order may be
           imperfect; document that macros should be defined
           before use in packages. v1 may expand with whatever
           macros are already in env (seed order / name order).
           Package seed should list macro files/bindings first
           when this matters.

         ==========================================================
         5. QUASIQUOTE EVALUATION MODEL
         ==========================================================

         quasiquote is a special form, not a macro on quote.

         qq(depth, template):
           depth starts at 1 when entering (quasiquote …)
           - (unquote e) at depth=1 -> eval e
           - (unquote e) at depth>1 -> rebuild unquote wrapper
             with qq(depth-1, e)  (nested quasiquote support)
           - (quasiquote e) -> rebuild with qq(depth+1, e)
           - (unquote-splicing e) at depth=1, list context ->
             eval e, must be list, splice
           - atoms / Vars / Lits at any depth -> quote_to_value
           - lists: map qq over elements; splicing flattens

         v1 MUST support single-level quasiquote well; nested
         quasiquote depth counting is required for correctness
         of macros-that-write-macros but can be tested lightly.

         Reader:
           `  introduces quasiquote of next expr
           ,  introduces unquote of next expr
           ,@ introduces unquote-splicing of next expr
         Prefer producing App(Var "quasiquote",[e]) etc. so
         existing App special-form path works; dedicated AST
         constructors (Quasiquote/Unquote/UnquoteSplicing) are
         allowed if JSON + pp + quote_to_value are updated in
         lockstep. Pick ONE representation in implementation
         and use it consistently (recommend App form for less
         JSON schema churn; dedicated constructors OK if cleaner).

         ==========================================================
         6. INTERACTION WITH EFFECTS / AI / BRANCHES
         ==========================================================

         Effects (lisp-interpreter.effects):
           - infer_effects runs on EXPANDED bodies when possible
           - quote / quasiquote templates contribute no runtime
             effects except through unquote subexpressions
           - defmacro body effects are Meta/code-time (top-level define persists via the session layer); the
             expanded call site carries the real effects
           - prim_effects: gensym = Pure; macroexpand* = Meta/Pure

         AI authoring (ai-authoring):
           - propose-function may emit defmacro and macro call
             sites; freeze path stores source AST as today
           - ast-rewrite tools edit source AST; expansion is
             re-done on next define/load

         Branches:
           Macros are bindings; they inherit/override via the
           same function_defs branch visibility as functions.
           A personal branch can override a system macro.

         ==========================================================
         7. ERRORS
         ==========================================================

           - arity mismatch on macro params (like lambda)
           - value_to_expr failure -> User_error with context
           - unquote outside quasiquote
           - unquote-splicing of non-list
           - defmacro/define of kernel special-form name
           - macro expansion budget exceeded
           - improper list from macro body (v1)

         ==========================================================
         8. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES
         ==========================================================

         Phase 1 — Bridge + quasiquote (no defmacro yet)
           - value_to_expr + round-trip tests with quote_to_value
           - reader sugar ` , ,@
           - eval quasiquote / unquote / unquote-splicing
           - special_forms catalog entries
           - gensym prim

         Phase 2 — defmacro + call-time expand
           - VMacro value
           - defmacro special form
           - App path: if operator evals to VMacro, expand
             (note: operator is a Var lookup without evaluating
              args — for (mac …) head is Var; do not eval head
              as function-before-expand in a way that forces
              args. Pattern: lookup Var name; if VMacro, expand;
              else existing eval head + eval args + apply.)
           - macroexpand-1 / macroexpand
           - tests: when/unless style macros

         Phase 3 — define/load expand + persistence
           - expand body on define before env install of lambdas
           - image load order: macros first
           - persist defmacro so reload restores VMacro
           - package seed: allow macros in libs/*

         Phase 4 — Product sugar (optional same PR / follow-up)
           - stdlib macros: when, unless, and, or, begin~seq alias
             if desired — only if they do not fight existing prims
           - UI: show macro badge on /bindings (kind=macro)

         ==========================================================
         9. SUCCESS CRITERIA
         ==========================================================

         - (defmacro when (t b) `(if ,t ,b ())) works in REPL
         - (when #t 42) => 42; (when #f 42) => ()
         - ,@ splices: `(a ,@'(b c) d) => (a b c d) as value list
         - quote/value_to_expr round-trip tests green
         - defmacro survives image restart / branch checkout
         - define of a function using macros expands so effects
           badges see real prims
         - dune build lisp + dune runtest lisp pass
         - No regression to pure-prim Lwt ABI

         ==========================================================
         10. DECISIONS LOCKED BY THIS SPEC
         ==========================================================

         - Name: defmacro (not define-macro)
         - Hygiene: unhygienic + gensym (no syntax-case)
         - Expansion: call-time always; ALSO expand-at-define/load
           for installed lambdas; store SOURCE AST
         - Symbol/string collapse REJECTED (subsection symbols).
           Do not build new macros that rely on collapse or __str__.
         - Quasiquote is a special form, not a user macro
         - Kernel special forms cannot be redefined as macros
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection primitives
   (doc "Primitives are the boundary between the pure interpreter and the outside world. Post-H1 the core is direct-style: prim_fn = value list -> value (lisp/lib/control.ml), and DB-touching prims are invoked via Control.perform_prim with the rim handler owning I/O — no Lwt.t in the prim ABI. The impure functions the interpreter calls into")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/primitives.ml lisp/lib/eval.ml lisp/lib/route_gate.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Primitives live in image/lib/primitives.ml. They
         are REAL OCaml, typed at the boundary, fast.
         Anything that touches Postgres, htmx, or the OS
         is a primitive, never user-Nopales.

         CRUD:
           (create 'type {fields...}) -> VRef
           (get 'type id) -> record value
           (update 'type id {fields...}) -> VRef
           (delete 'type id) -> VNil
           (select-for-update 'type id) -> record (locked)

         Branching:
           (fork parent child-name) -> branch-id
           (merge source target) -> status
           (current-branch) -> branch-id
           (checkout branch-id) -> VNil (* session switches *)
           (list-branches) -> VList
           (diff source target) -> VList of changes

         htmx/Routes:
           (route path fn)            (* register route *)
           (render-html node)          (* pure-html node *)
           (hx-get url) (hx-post url) (* attribute helpers *)

         Reflection:
           (type-of value) -> type-tag


           (list-bindings) -> VList

         Sessions:
           (whoami) -> username
           (current-author) -> username (* for perm checks *)

         All primitives that touch data consult the
         CURRENT-AUTHOR (from the binding being executed)
         for permission filtering, per multi-user model.

         Effect tags for each prim are defined in
         lisp-interpreter.effects (capability lattice).
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection effects
   (doc "Capability effects for Nopales programs: classify pure vs DB vs AI vs publish; infer; gate; display. Separate from OCaml 5 host effects and from Lwt async plumbing.")
   (implements lisp/lib/effect.ml lisp/lib/eval.ml pure/prims_pure.ml)
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (build "dune build lisp image")
    (test "effect inference pure vs db vs ai on sample ASTs")
    (smoke "/bindings shows effect badges; AI propose rejects disallowed effects when policy on"))

   (details
    (doc (|
         ==========================================================
         0. THREE PROBLEMS PEOPLE CONFUSE (read this first)
         ==========================================================

         A) ASYNC / COMPOSITION  (mostly DONE)
            Can a function call (get ...) and use the result?
            YES today:
              (define u (get 'note id))
              (dict-get u "title")
            Mechanism: eval : env -> expr -> value Lwt.t
            Pure prims: register_sync wraps Lwt.return
            DB/AI prims: return promises directly
            This is the "async problem" for data-in-Nopales.
            It is runtime plumbing, not an effect system.

         B) CAPABILITY EFFECTS  (NOT DONE — this section)
            What powers does this function need?
            Pure calc vs read DB vs write DB vs AI vs publish page.
            Needed for: UI badges, AI safety, page policy,
            testing without services, author-runs-as-author story.

         C) HOST ALGEBRAIC EFFECTS (OPTIONAL LATER)
            OCaml 5 Effect.perform / handlers inside the
            interpreter implementation. Could one day replace
            some Lwt coloring. Does NOT give Nopales programs
            effects for free. Guest language still needs B.

         Do not block B on C. Do not re-solve A while building B.

         ==========================================================
         1. WHY EFFECTS
         ==========================================================

         Today every lambda looks the same:
           (lambda (x) (* x x))              ; pure
           (lambda () (get 'note id))        ; read DB
           (lambda () (create 'note "{}"))   ; write DB
           (lambda () (propose-function ...)); AI + write code
           (lambda () (render-html "..."))   ; page-ish

         The OS cares which is which. Effects make that
         first-class, inspectable, enforceable.

         ==========================================================
         2. EFFECT LATTICE (capability sets)
         ==========================================================

         Base labels (finite, small):

           Pure      no capability prims; only pure computation
           ReadDb    get, rows, select-for-update, list-branches, ...
           WriteDb   create, update, update-field, delete, deftype, ...
           Branch    fork, merge, checkout
           Publish   route (and later define-page helpers)
           Ai        propose-function / LLM client
           Html      render-html (soft; display intent, not security)
           Txn       body expects with-transaction scope


         A function's effect set is a SET of labels, e.g.
           {ReadDb, Html}
           {WriteDb, Ai}
           {}

         Ordering for policy (allowlist checks):
           Pure ⊂ anything
           ReadDb ⊂ ReadDb∪WriteDb  (writes imply reads usually)
           optional sugar: Db = ReadDb∪WriteDb

         NOTE on (define ...):
           Top-level define persists code — a code-store write
           performed by the session layer, NOT an effect tracked
           in the lattice. Distinguish:
             - effects OF a function body (what it does when called)
             - effects OF defining it (session install + persist)

         ==========================================================
         3. PRIM EFFECT TABLE (initial map)
         ==========================================================

           Pure:
             + - * = < > <= >= not
             list car cdr cons null? pair? count
             string-append str type-of
             dict-get dict-set dict-keys
             hx-get hx-post          ; string tags only

           ReadDb:
             get rows select-for-update
             list-branches current-branch list-bindings
             whoami current-author   ; session read; treat ReadDb or soft Ambient

           WriteDb:
             create update update-field delete deftype

           Branch:
             fork merge checkout

           Publish:
             route

           Html:
             render-html

           Ai:
             propose-function
             (Ai usually pairs with WriteDb on code store via define path)

           Txn-related:
             with-transaction (special form) introduces Txn scope
             select-for-update meaningful only under Txn

         Image layer owns the table (primitives live in image/).
         Nopales package may define the effect datatype only.

         ==========================================================
         4. INFERENCE (syntactic, conservative)
         ==========================================================

         effects(expr) =
           union over subexpressions
           + effects of any prim head used in App
           + effects of known global bindings referenced
             (lookup function_defs metadata / env annotation)

         Rules of thumb:
           - overapproximate: if unsure, add effect
           - higher-order unknown fn => Effects.top or {ReadDb,WriteDb,Ai,Publish,Branch} policy knob
           - lambda body effects = function effects
           - quote bodies contribute no runtime effects
           - quasiquote: only unquote / unquote-splicing
             subexpressions contribute; template skeleton is Pure
           - infer on EXPANDED bodies when macros are present
             (lisp-interpreter.macros); macro heads themselves
             are not prims

         On successful (define name body):
           store inferred effect set next to function
           (new column function_defs.effects jsonb OR side table OR
            derive on the fly from body AST — prefer derive-on-read
            for v1 to avoid migration, cache later)

         v1 implementation preference:
           derive-on-read from AST + prim table (no schema change)
           optional cache field later

         ==========================================================
         5. GATES / POLICY (where effects pay rent)
         ==========================================================

         a) UI badges
            /bindings and editor show: pure | db-read | db-write | ai | page

         b) AI propose policy (default)
            allowed = {Pure, ReadDb, Html}
            denied  = {WriteDb, Branch, Publish, Ai} unless user elevates
            (tune experimentally; document in product-experience)

         c) Route publish policy (optional v1)
            (route path fn) warns or rejects if fn has Branch/Ai
            pages should be ReadDb/Html/Pure by default

         d) Sandbox eval (future)
            browser anonymous do-it: Pure only

         e) Transactions
            if body has select-for-update or multi WriteDb,
            linter suggests with-transaction

         ==========================================================
         6. ASYNC STORY (what is already true)
         ==========================================================

         Solved:
           - eval returns value Lwt.t
           - prim_fn = value list -> value Lwt.t
           - composition of DB reads in Nopales works
           - with-transaction threads dynamic connection

         Not the same as effects:
           Lwt answers "when / how concurrency is scheduled"
           Effects answer "what capabilities are required"

         Example:
           (get 'note id) is BOTH
             async (returns promise through eval)
             AND ReadDb (capability)

         You already need both; only async exists today.

         ==========================================================
         7. RELATION TO OCAML 5 EFFECTS
         ==========================================================

         OCaml 5 algebraic effects MAY later implement host-side
         intercept for DB/AI inside the interpreter (handlers).
         That is a runtime rewrite of image/lisp internals.

         Explicit non-goals for first effects delivery:
           - do not replace Lwt in Mirage/httpaf/pgx stack
           - do not expose Effect.perform to Nopales users
           - do not require monadic syntax in Nopales

         Guest programs keep looking like normal Nopales.
         Effects are metadata + checks, not new call syntax
         (unless we add optional declare forms later).

         Optional sugar later:
           (define-pure name body)
           (define-page path body)
         Desugar to define + check inferred ⊆ declared.

         ==========================================================
         8. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES
         ==========================================================

         Phase 0 (docs/UI copy): explain Pure vs DB vs AI in /about
         Phase 1: Effect module + prim -> effect set table
         Phase 2: infer_effects : expr -> EffectSet.t
         Phase 3: badges on /bindings + /edit + inspect
         Phase 4: AI propose allowlist gate (configurable)
         Phase 5: route publish warnings
         Phase 6: session capability masks + ENFORCEMENT
           (see capability-enforcement; language-roadmap Phase 3)
         Phase 7 (optional research): OCaml effect handlers under eval

         NOTE: Phases 1–5 of THIS effects subsection are largely
         done (table, infer, badges, AI gate). Roadmap Phase 3 is
         about turning metadata into hard gates on public routes
         and agent sessions — not redoing inference.

         ==========================================================
         9. DATA SHAPES (sketch)
         ==========================================================

         type effect =
           | Pure | ReadDb | WriteDb | Branch
           | Publish | Ai | Html | Txn

         type effect_set = effect list  (* unique sorted *)

         val prim_effects : string -> effect_set
         val infer : prim_table_meta -> expr -> effect_set
         val subset : effect_set -> effect_set -> bool
         val pp : effect_set -> string  (* "pure" / "read-db+html" *)

         No change to value or eval types required for Phases 1-5.

         ==========================================================
         10. SUCCESS CRITERIA
         ==========================================================

         - User can see whether a function is pure or touches DB/AI
         - AI-proposed functions can be policy-gated by effects
         - Pure functions remain testable without Postgres
         - Async composition remains intact (no regression of Lwt ABI)
         - Spec/docs stop conflating async with effects
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection binding-sugar
   (doc "Define and body ergonomics: function define, begin/multi-body")
   (status implemented)
   (implements lisp/lib/eval.ml lisp/lib/special_forms.ml)
   (verify
    (test "(define (f x) (+ x 1)) installs lambda; (f 2) => 3")
    (test "multi-body define evaluates to last form")
    (test "(begin e1 e2 … en) => en")
    (test "old (define name (lambda …)) still works")
    (test "define docstring forms coexist (see define-docstring)"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         SHIPPED.

         FUNCTION DEFINE SUGAR
           (define (name param…) ["doc"] body body* …)
             ≡ (define name (lambda (param…) (begin body…)))
           Single body may omit begin. Dotted rest params: out of
           scope (User_error if "." appears in param list).

           Still valid:
             (define name expr)
             (define name (lambda …))
             (define name expr "doc")

         BEGIN / MULTI-BODY
           (begin e1 … en) and (seq e1 … en) — special forms;
           value of en; earlier forms for effect. body_of_exprs
           desugars multi-body define/lambda/match/guard arms to
           Seq chains.

         PRINT
           function-source pretty-prints stored AST; short-form
           (define (f x) …) preference is cosmetic when body is
           Lambda — optional, not required for this section.

         NON-GOALS
           - Internal define
           - let function sugar
           - defmacro (name p…) sugar (follow-up if needed)

         Docstrings: define-docstring (orthogonal, implemented).
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection optional-rest-args
   (doc "Optional and rest lambda parameters: &optional with defaults, &rest collection. Backward compatible with fixed arity.")
   (implements lisp/lib/eval.ml lisp/lib/special_forms.ml lisp/lib/reader.ml)
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-05: implemented &optional (default "") and &rest markers in apply_step. No &optional defaults yet (BORGE-1785965096). 8 new eval tests pass; 136 total green. dune build clean.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build lisp")
    (test "dune runtest — lisp eval optional/rest cases")
    (test "(lambda (a &optional b) b) called with 1 arg: b is unbound/empty")
    (test "(lambda (a &optional b) b) called with 2 args: b is 2nd arg")
    (test "(lambda (&rest args) args) collects all args into a list")
    (test "(lambda (a &rest rest) rest) collects extras after required")
    (test "fixed-arity lambdas still enforce exact arity (backward compat)"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         House style stays FIXED ARITY for most image code. This
         subsection adds two backward-compatible extensions so user
         code stops reinventing optional/keyword args via empty-string
         sentinels and opts dicts.

         SYNTAX (in the parameter list, after required params):
           (lambda (a b &optional c) body)
             — c is optional; if omitted, bound to empty string "".
           (lambda (a b &optional (c default)) body)
             — c is optional; if omitted, bound to default (evaluated
               in the call env at bind time).
           (lambda (a b &rest args) body)
             — args is bound to a VList of all remaining arguments.
           (lambda (&rest all) body)
             — all-variadic; all args collected.

         &optional and &rest may not both appear. &rest must be the
         last marker. These are markers (symbols matching the literal
         strings "&optional" / "&rest"), not params.

         DOTTED PAIR NOTATION (a . b) remains UNSUPPORTED at the
         reader level (parse error). The &rest marker is the only way
         to collect variadic args. This keeps the reader simple.

         ARITY SEMANTICS
           Required params: must all be supplied.
           &optional: supplied or fewer — no error; missing ones get
             their default (or "" if no default form).
           &rest: absorbs any extra args beyond required+optional.
           Calling with FEWER than required → Arity_mismatch (as today).
           Calling with MORE than required+optional (and no &rest) →
             Arity_mismatch (as today).

         IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
           - The Lambda constructor / VLambda carry a param spec richer
             than string list: required params, optional (name, default
             expr option) list, rest name option. A flat string list
             with &optional/&rest markers as sentinels is the minimal
             change; a typed param_spec is cleaner but larger.
           - apply_step binds required first (arity check against
             min_arity), then optional (fill defaults for missing),
             then rest (collect leftover into VList).
           - params_of_define_sugar must accept &optional/&rest markers
             and stop raising on their presence (the current '.' check
             stays — dotted notation is still rejected).
           - pp_expr / value_to_expr round-trip must preserve the
             param list shape so function-source prints correctly.

         NON-GOALS
           - &key keyword args (opts-dict convention stays until a real
             need proves it; Smalltalk keyword messages are the model).
           - Dotted-pair (a . b) reader syntax (use &rest).
           - Optional/rest on macros (follow-up; macros already have
             their own arity path — add later if needed).
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection define-docstring
   (doc "Optional docstring on define; persist to function_defs.doc; (function-doc name)")
   (implements lisp/lib/eval.ml lisp/lib/special_forms.ml image/lib/session.ml image/lib/store.ml image/lib/agent_tools.ml image/lib/primitives.ml)
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (build "dune build && dune runtest")
    (test "(define d1 1 \"one\") binds 1; (function-doc \"d1\") => \"one\"")
    (test "(define (inc x) \"add one\" (+ x 1)); (inc 2) => 3; function-doc has docstring")
    (test "(define f (lambda (x) \"lam\" (+ x 1))); function-doc and body without dead string")
    (test "redefine without docstring clears or leaves prior doc per documented rule")
    (test "(function-doc \"missing\") => ()")
    (test "old two-arg define still works; load-library packages without docs")
    (smoke "function_defs.doc round-trips across persist + lookup")
    (smoke "fork/merge copy doc with body"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         PURPOSE
           Make every binding documentable in-language so agents
           and humans can build a live reference (apropos, /ref,
           platform-ref notes) without a parallel docs system.
           Inbox SPEC "define docstring" is the product brief;
           this subsection is the book authority for the OCaml
           kernel half. Nopales package doc coverage, /ref, and
           apropos are follow-on product work once this lands.

         SCHEMA (already present — no migration)
           function_defs.doc text  — see storage.schema /
           migrations/001_branches.sql. Column exists; writers
           and readers historically ignored it. This work wires
           it. Do NOT add a second description column.

         SURFACE SYNTAX (all optional doc)
           1. (define name expr)
                no docstring; bind eval(expr)
           2. (define name expr "doc")
                bind eval(expr); doc = the string literal
           3. (define (name p…) body…)
                sugar → lambda; no doc unless (4)
           4. (define (name p…) "doc" body body*…)
                leading string literal in the body list is the
                docstring and is NOT part of the evaluated body
                (requires at least one real body form after it;
                 a lone string body is a value-returning thunk,
                 not a docstring)
           5. (define name (lambda (p…) "doc" body…))
                same leading-string rule inside lambda bodies
                when the define is top-level: strip into doc and
                store/bind the lambda WITHOUT the dead string so
                function-source stays clean

           Docstring MUST be a string literal (Lit VStr / reader
           "…"). A computed expression in the doc slot is a
           syntax error (User_error), not silently evaluated.

         EVAL (pure lisp/)
           - Accept forms (1)–(5) without error.
           - Binding value is unchanged vs pre-docstring define.
           - Pure env does not store docs (no env metadata in v1).
           - Prefer one shared helper (e.g. split_define /
             peel_docstring) used by eval and by the image
             intercept so sugar and doc rules cannot drift.
           - value_to_expr / quote of Define need not round-trip
             the doc string inside the AST (doc is store metadata,
             not part of expr). Body AST after peel has no doc.

         IMAGE PERSIST
           session eval_one / eval_form define intercept and
           agent_tools.define_binding:
             - peel name, body_expr, doc option
             - eval as today (env bind)
             - Store.persist_function … ?doc
           Redefine rules:
             - docstring present → write that string to doc
             - docstring absent → set doc NULL (explicit clear).
               Rationale: edit/save and load-library without docs
               must not leave stale blurbs from an older body.
               Packages that care keep the string in source.
           Anonymous may not persist (unchanged).
           defmacro: out of scope for v1 docstring slot (no
           third-arg / leading-string capture); follow-up if needed.

         STORE
           persist_function : t -> name -> body -> author ->
             ?doc:string option -> unit Lwt.t
           INSERT/UPDATE sets doc (NULL when None).
           lookup_function / list paths that feed function-doc
           must SELECT fd.doc (nullable).
           fork copy, merge upsert, promote_functions: copy doc
           with body/author so docs survive branch ops.

         PRIMITIVE (function-doc name) → string | ()
           - name: string (binding name)
           - If visible function_defs row has non-null non-empty
             doc → VStr doc
           - Else if name is a special form → that form's summary
             from special_forms catalog (discoverability)
           - Else if name is a registered prim with a help blurb
             → that blurb when one exists
           - Else VNil
           Effect class: ReadDb (store lookup); pure fallbacks
           after miss are fine inside the same prim.
           Register next to function-source / function-ast.
           package-exports kernel/std list should include
           function-doc once shipped.

         SPECIAL-FORMS CATALOG
           Update define entry arity/examples to show optional
           docstring forms.

         NON-GOALS (this subsection)
           - /ref page, apropos, nav link, ai/context wiring
             (product docs layer; build after kernel works)
           - Mass docstring backfill of libs/*.pp
           - doc on type_defs or routes
           - Markdown rendering of docs in OCaml UI (plain text)
           - Changing function_defs PRIMARY KEY or body JSON shape

         SUCCESS
           - One define with a docstring round-trips through
             persist and (function-doc …)
           - Sugar and lambda leading-string forms agree
           - Existing libs without docs load unchanged
           - Agents can document bindings in-language and read
             them back without scraping function-source comments
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection match
   (doc "Pattern match / destructuring for lists and code-as-data trees")
   (status implemented)
   (implements lisp/lib/eval.ml)
   (verify
    (test "match list pattern binds vars")
    (test "match symbolic head (lambda params body) with VSymbol")
    (test "match fallthrough / else via _")
    (test "no match raises catchable User_error"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         SHIPPED special form:

           (match scrutinee
             (pat1 body1…)
             (pat2 body2…)
             (_     else-body…))

         Patterns (v1):
           _                 wildcard
           x                 bind variable
           lit               number / bool / nil / string
           'sym              quoted symbol (VSymbol; VStr compat)
           "str"             string literal
           (head p…)         list starting with symbolic/string head
           (list p…)         explicit list pattern

         Semantics:
           First matching clause wins. Scrutinee eval once.
           Bindings lexical in clause body (Env.child).
           No match => User_error "match: no matching clause"
           (catchable via guard).

         Non-goals v1 (deferred):
           dotted-tail patterns, (? pred p), and/or patterns,
           exhaustiveness, record patterns, regexp.

         SUCCESS: idiomatic AST walk over read-string / function-ast
         trees with symbolic heads works without car/cdr chains.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection math
   (doc "Numeric tower beyond the four operations: expt/sqrt/trig/log, numeric predicates, integer division aliases, seeded RNG")
   (status implemented)
   (implements pure/prims_pure.ml image/lib/primitives.ml image/lib/session.ml lisp/lib/effect.ml image/test/primitives_test.ml lisp/test/effect_test.ml)
   (verify
    (build "dune build lisp image")
    (test "expt/sqrt/log/exp/sin/cos/tan round-trip on known values (float results)")
    (test "(expt 2 10) => 1024 int-in int-out; (expt 2.0 0.5) => 1.414… float")
    (test "number?/integer?/zero?/even?/odd? classify VInt/VFloat/VStr/VNil correctly")
    (test "quotient/remainder match div/mod on ints incl. negative dividends")
    (test "(random n) => 0 ≤ r < n; (random) => float in [0,1); seeded random deterministic per sequence")
    (test "div-by-zero and domain errors (sqrt of negative, log of ≤0) raise catchable User_error")
    (test "conformance_test.ml pins at least one case per new prim"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         GAP. Core 1.0 froze the four operations (+ - * div / mod),
         abs/min/max, floor/round, and parse-int/parse-float — but
         nothing else. Anything numeric beyond grade-school
         arithmetic currently has to shell out to float-of-string
         hacks or OCaml. This subsection is the book authority for
         closing that gap. Classified ADDITIVE under core-freeze.

         NEW PRIMS (all pure, all in pure/prims_pure.ml (the shared
         pure library), registered in primitives.ml, added to the
         Pure list in lisp/lib/effect.ml; note the Pure list today omits "/"
         even though div is listed — fix that omission in the same
         pass so alias pairs (div /) and (quotient remainder)
         stay classified identically)

         Powers / roots / transcendental (float semantics;
         int-int expt with non-negative exponent returns VInt):
           (expt base n)   int-in/int-out when both int and n ≥ 0
                           (Int64 overflow wraps like Int64.mul;
                           documented, no bignum in v1); else float
           (sqrt x)        float; User_error on x < 0
           (exp x) (log x) float; log: User_error on x ≤ 0
           (log x b)       optional base arg → (logb x) = log x / log b
           (sin x) (cos x) (tan x)     float, radians
           (asin x) (acos x) (atan x)  float; asin/acos User_error
                                       outside [-1,1]
           (atan y x)      two-arg form → atan2 when given 2 args
           pi              bound as a VALUE (VFloat Float.pi) in the
                           initial env, not a zero-arg prim — agents
                           write pi, not (pi)

         Predicates (return VBool; missing core gap):
           (number? v)   VInt | VFloat
           (integer? v)  VInt only; VFloat with integral value
                         returns #f (v1 rule: tag-based, not
                         value-based — keeps it cheap and honest)
           (zero? n) (positive? n) (negative? n)
           (even? n) (odd? n)   integers only; User_error on float
                                with non-integral value

         Integer division aliases (Scheme names, delegate to the
         existing div/mod int paths — no new semantics):
           (quotient a b)   ≡ int div; User_error on float args or 0
           (remainder a b)  ≡ Int64.rem; User_error on float args or 0

         Randomness:
           (random n)      VInt in [0,n) for VInt n > 0;
                           VFloat in [0.,1.) for zero args
           (random-seed s) reseeds the image PRNG (Random.init with
                           VInt-derived seed); returns VNil
           Effect class: Pure — Nopales-visible nondeterminism,
           but touches no capability rim; same class as now/now-ms.
           One PRNG state per image process is fine for v1
           (documented; per-session streams are a non-goal).

         EFFECT CLASSIFICATION
           All new prims are Pure. Quotient/remainder/classification
           aliases must classify identically to the prim they wrap
           (div / mod) — alias drift in the Pure list is a drift
           failure mode; the conformance suite should catch a
           prim that infers non-Pure.

         ERROR RULES (uniform)
           - Wrong arity / non-numeric arg → User_error naming prim
           - Domain errors (sqrt/log/asin/acos, div-by-zero) →
             catchable User_error, never VNil, never NaN leak:
             prims CHECK before calling the OCaml float op
           - integer? on VFloat 3.0 → #f (tag rule above)

         NON-GOALS (v1)
           - Rationals / exact arithmetic tower
           - Bignums (Int64 wraps; documented)
           - Complex numbers
           - (gcd a b) (lcm a b) — useful but trivially writable
             in Nopales once quotient/remainder exist; candidates
             for libs/std, not kernel prims
           - Per-session RNG streams

         SUCCESS: agents and product libs can do real math
         (interest, FSRS scheduling, geometry, statistics)
         without leaving the language; conformance suite pins
         every new name so silent removal reddens the build.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection std-library
   (doc "Blessed std list/string helpers: freeze surface for packages and agents")
   (status implemented)
   (implements lisp/lib/eval.ml pure/prims_pure.ml libs/std/lib.pp lisp/test/conformance_test.ml)
   (verify
    (test "conformance covers list/map filter foldl foldr")
    (test "symbol and string prims present")
    (smoke "load-library std exports list helpers; idempotent"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         RULES
           1. FROZEN SET below — signatures must not change in place;
              bugfixes OK; new behavior needs a new name.
           2. Hot loops: OCaml special forms / prims; Nopales std wraps.
           3. New std helpers require a conformance or verify case.

         FROZEN SET (kernel + image prims + libs/std aliases)

           Lists (kernel special forms — OCaml loops + apply):
             list/map list/filter list/foldl list/foldr
           Lists (prims):
             list car cdr cons null? pair? reverse append count
           Strings (prims):
             string-append string-join string-length substring string-eq
             string-contains? string-trim html-escape
             as-str str
           Symbols (prims):
             symbol? string? symbol=? symbol->string string->symbol
           Misc:
             not type-of

           libs/std also exports ergonomic aliases map/filter/foldl/
           foldr and html/* builders — html/* may evolve faster than
           this kernel freeze; list HOF names above are frozen.

         SUCCESS: conformance + dialect tests pin list HOF and
         symbol/string prims; silent renames redden the suite.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection capability-enforcement
   (doc "Hard gates: public routes and agent sessions obey effect allowlists")
   (status implemented)
   (implements lisp/lib/route_gate.ml lisp/lib/effect.ml image/lib/session.ml)
   (verify
    (test "pure and read-db public handlers allowed")
    (test "write-db / branch / net public handlers denied")
    (test "unknown and non-function handlers fail-closed")
    (test "macro-expanded write is denied after expand")
    (smoke "(route … 'public) refuses over-powered handlers at bind"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         SHIPPED (public-route gate). Builds on effects inference.

         PUBLIC ROUTES
           Route_gate.check_public_handler ~env handler:
             infer effects of the named handler from the in-memory
             env (macroexpand first). Allowlist default:
               Pure | ReadDb | Html
             WriteDb requires PP_PUBLIC_ALLOW_WRITE=1 opt-in.
             Branch / Ai / Net / unknown → deny (fail-closed).
           Session.route bind calls the gate when visibility=public;
           over-powered handlers are refused at bind (kept private).

         AGENT / AI
           AI propose-function remains policy-gated on the same
           effect subset helpers. Per-session agent effect_mask for
           every tool prim is a follow-on tightening (not required
           to close this section's public-route gate).

         BUDGET
           Eval step budget is a hard leash (Budget_exceeded),
           uncatchable by default via guard — part of the same
           production-leash story.

         AUTH
           Orthogonal to is_admin: admin private routes full power;
           public publish still PUBLIC_ALLOW.

         NON-GOALS
           - Full setuid / run-as-author lattice
           - Postgres RLS as effects substitute
           - Required user-authored effect declarations

         SUCCESS: sloppy public handlers cannot fork branches or
         write the store without opt-in; pure unit gate tests green.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection package-surface
   (doc "Blessed package exports and stability rules for system/main")
   (status implemented)
   (implements
    migrations/011_package_registry.sql
    image/lib/store.ml
    image/lib/tree_store.ml
    image/lib/session.ml
    image/lib/session_types.ml
    image/lib/agent_tools.ml
    libs/std/lib.pp
    libs/ui/lib.pp
    libs/layout/lib.pp
    libs/todo/lib.pp)
   (* agent note (|agent plan 2026-08-02 — Phase 1 design settled with roerick.
      Resolves most of BORGE-1785211142 item a; multi-file libs issue item b
      stays deferred. Design calls settled in chat:

      SETTLED DESIGN CALLS
      1. Export names are FULL names like todo/add, not bare auto-prefixed.
      2. Registry storage = new branch-lensed tree family
         b.branch.pkg.name one-tree.tree-model PATH GRAMMAR updated
         with a package_registry scaffolding table mirroring it, exactly
         the function_defs pattern table = runtime truth until
         PP_TREE_READS/WRITES cutover; forward-only mirror triggers.
         Inherits parent-to-child like function_defs — per-user
         enable/disable falls out of the branch model for free.
      3. std stays ALWAYS enabled — hard-protected; not a registry row.
         package-exports std keeps serving kernel_std_exports until
         the std-library freeze lands; do not widen that list in passing.
      4. Routes / deftype / macros are OUT of the export list for v1
         functions only.
      5. Personal branches may still shadow exported names; documented,
         not prevented.

      DISABLE SEMANTICS = HIDE, NOT DELETE. package-disable writes
      enabled=false on the current branch and re-runs rehydrate; rows
      stay in Postgres, they just do not load into the session env.
      Boot seed is automatically safe: seed is additive fills MISSING
      names and disabled rows still exist, so seeding a disabled
      package is a no-op. Re-enable flips the bit back; no re-seed.

      MVP PRIM SURFACE
        packages                    -- registry rows: name, enabled,
                                    -- exports, source header-or-heuristic
        package-enable workout      -- enabled=true on current branch,
                                    -- rehydrate
        package-disable workout     -- enabled=false, rehydrate.
                                    -- std rejected with a clear error.
        package-exports pkg         -- resolution order: declared header
                                    -- set, then kernel_std_exports for
                                    -- std and kernel, else shallow heuristic

      IMPLEMENTATION PLAN touchpoints verified 2026-08-02
      1. migrations/011_package_registry.sql — package_registry
         fields branch_id, name, enabled bool default true, exports jsonb,
         author, updated_at; PK branch_id+name; plus forward-only
         mirror trigger copying 009_tree.sql tree_mirror_function_defs
         pattern pp.tree_mirror guard, CONSTRAINT TRIGGER DEFERRABLE.
         Forward-only is correct: 009 has NO reverse trigger; runtime
         truth stays in the table. Also ALTER TABLE function_defs
         ADD COLUMN package text NULL provenance stamp.
      2. tree_store.ml — pkg_path constructor b.uuid.pkg.name
         via uuid_label + enc_label, mirrors tree_uuid_label in SQL.
      3. loader.ml / session.ml — recognize a leading package NAME
         export ... header form in load-string; do NOT eval it as a
         define. Upsert the registry row enabled preserved on re-load;
         exports replaced. While loading that file, stamp
         persist_function calls with package=NAME session-local
         current-package string option threaded through eval_one /
         eval_form persist_define; cleared at end of load.
      4. session.ml rehydrate/load_defs — after loading the registry
         for the branch chain same recursive-CTE override walk as
         list_function_names_for_load, first-hit-wins depth ASC,
         skip defines whose package is disabled on this branch.
         Untagged defines package IS NULL always load. Routes whose
         handler belongs to a disabled package are filtered in
         load_defs route pass too handler package via
         function_defs.package.
      5. agent_tools.ml / session.ml prims — packages, package-enable,
         package-disable new; package-exports resolution order
         change; describe prefers declared exports when present.
      6. Seed headers: libs/std/lib.pp exports = kernel_std_exports
         list exactly, libs/ui/lib.pp, libs/layout/lib.pp; todo as
         the app example.
      7. export-package round-trips the package header form into
         vault text; pp-sync snapshot includes it.

      EXPLICIT NON-GOALS FOR PHASE 1 unchanged from details above:
      sealing, import forms, multi-file libs, requires graph, promote
      filter, call-site lint. Those are Phase 2/3.

      VERIFY maps to the smoke lines on this subsection
      - package-disable todo then todo/list -> unbound error;
        rows still in function_defs; package-enable todo restores.
      - Disabled on admin/personal does not affect system/main.
      - package-exports todo returns the declared header set, not
        the shallow heuristic, after seeding the header.
      - Boot with a package disabled on system/main: seed does NOT
        re-enable it.
      - borge drift clean; dune build and dune runtest.
   |) *)
   (verify
    (smoke "export-package or package-bindings lists public names for std/ui")
    (smoke "docs or /bindings marks public vs internal"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ROADMAP: Phase 3 (ecosystem law, light).

         ==========================================================
         PROBLEM
         ==========================================================
           system/main packages are de facto public API for all
           gardens. No export list → agents depend on internals;
           renames break personal branches silently.

         ==========================================================
         v1 RULES (no heavy module system)
         ==========================================================
           1. Each libs/<pkg>/lib.pp MAY start with a declaration:
                (package pkg-name
                  (export name1 name2 …))
              If absent, all defines are public (status quo).
              If present, non-exported names are "internal":
                still callable (Lisp-1) but documented as unstable;
                optional lint/warn on other packages referencing them.

           2. Stability tiers:
                kernel prims     — strongest freeze (with std-library)
                exported package — prefer compat; rename = new name
                internal         — may change any deploy
                personal branch  — user owns breakage

           3. Promote discipline (existing branching.promote):
                only exported names should be promoted to main
                without explicit force.

           4. Agent seeds: prefer exported names only.

         ==========================================================
         NON-GOALS
         ==========================================================
           - Import forms / separate namespaces per file
           - Version numbers in-package (git/branch is version)
           - Sealing that makes internal uncallable (too hostile
             for a Lisp OS v1)

         SUCCESS: std + ui + layout have export lists; concepts
         doc explains public vs internal; agents stop depending
         on clearly internal helpers by default.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection function-observability
   (doc "Inspect functions: recursion/tail highlight, step counts, DB cost, big-O heuristic, on-demand LLM analysis, inside-out peel")
   (status implemented)
   (implements
    lisp/lib/recursion.ml
    lisp/lib/complexity.ml
    lisp/lib/explain.ml
    lisp/lib/eval.ml
    lisp/test/recursion_test.ml
    image/lib/ast_html.ml
    image/lib/db_cost.ml
    image/lib/analyze.ml
    image/lib/http_analyze.ml
    image/lib/call_graph.ml
    image/lib/http_pages.ml
    image/lib/prims_db.ml
    image/lib/session.ml
    image/lib/llm_client.ml)
   (verify
    (build "dune build lisp image")
    (test "dune runtest — recursion / complexity / explain / eval step attribution")
    (smoke "GET /binding/<recursive-fn> marks self-ref and tail vs non-tail sites")
    (smoke "GET /binding/<fn> shows last-run steps / prim_calls / db_hits when stats exist")
    (smoke "GET /binding/<fn> shows free big-O badge or honest ?")
    (smoke "POST /api/analyze/<fn> returns LLM estimate fragment; cache by AST sha1")
    (smoke "(analyze 'fn) and (explain 'fn) prims return structured results"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ==========================================================
         THESIS
         ==========================================================
           The OS already stores every function as an AST and runs
           it through a stepped evaluator. Function observability
           makes that inspectable on /binding and /bindings without
           inventing a separate profiler product.

           Split by cost:
             FREE / pure sexp    — recursion, tail position, big-O
                                  heuristic, inside-out peel structure
             CHEAP / eval hook  — step counts, prim calls, DB hits,
                                  live DB timing into function_stat
             ON DEMAND / LLM    — (analyze 'fn) complexity + optional
                                  per-layer prose glosses

         ==========================================================
         ARCHITECTURE
         ==========================================================
           lisp/ stays pure (yojson only). Static analysis modules
           live there and are alcotest-tested with no services:
             recursion.ml   — self-refs + tail context
             complexity.ml  — heuristic big-O classifier
             explain.ml     — mechanical innermost→out peel

           image/ owns HTML, store, LLM:
             ast_html.ml     — Pure_html AST renderer with classes
             db_cost.ml      — static DB-prim cost tiers + optional EXPLAIN
             analyze.ml      — (analyze 'fn) + cache write
             http_analyze.ml — POST /api/analyze/:name htmx fragment

           Instrumentation seam is the existing eval budget record
           and current_budget dynamic binding (eval.ml). Extend budget
           with current_fn, prim_calls, db_hits, fn_step_counts; expose
           last_budget after top-level eval_string for session persist.

           Analysis results dogfood data_rows:
             function_stat       — last/recent measured runs
             function_analysis   — LLM complexity cache keyed by
                                   sha1(pp_expr body); edit invalidates

         ==========================================================
         PHASE 1 — RECURSION + TAIL HIGHLIGHT
         ==========================================================
           Recursion.analyze_self ~fn_name expr:
             is_recursive, tail_recursive, self_ref_sites,
             mutual_refs (names only; cycles from call_graph).
           Tail context MUST mirror the trampoline (eval TCO):
             If then/else Tail; test Non_tail
             Seq last Tail; earlier Non_tail
             Let/Letrec body Tail; bindings Non_tail
             App in Tail context is the tail call; args Non_tail
             Lambda body is a new frame (Non_tail of the caller)
           call_graph.mutual_cycles on a snapshot for SCCs.
           ast_html.pp_expr_html emits spans:
             ast-self-ref, ast-tail, ast-non-tail, ast-mutual
           Wire show_binding_page, list_bindings code pane,
           inspect_binding_page. CSS in chrome style block.

         ==========================================================
         PHASE 2 — STEP COUNTER + ATTRIBUTION
         ==========================================================
           Every eval_step tick attributes to budget.current_fn.
           apply of named VLambda sets current_fn; non-tail eval
           restores previous; trampoline tail keeps new name.
           Prim path increments prim_calls; CRUD wrappers in
           prims_db increment db_hits (and later timing).
           After route/api eval, session may upsert function_stat
           rows (fn, steps, prim_calls, db_hits, when).
           /binding shows "last run: N steps, M prims, K DB hits".

         ==========================================================
         PHASE 3 — DB COST
         ==========================================================
           Closed set of DB prims → static tier badges:
             get/find-row Index_seek; create/update/delete Constant;
             rows Seq_scan; find-rows/find-text Linear.
           Optional lazy EXPLAIN on demand (not every page load).
           Live tier: wrap store calls with wall timing into
           function_stat.db_time_ms when clock precision allows.

         ==========================================================
         PHASE 4 — HEURISTIC BIG-O
         ==========================================================
           Complexity.classify : expr -> O_constant | O_log_n |
             O_linear | O_n_log_n | O_quadratic | O_exponential |
             Unknown
           Patterns (honest Unknown default):
             cdr-self-recursion / list/map|filter|foldl → O(n)
             two recursive halves → O(n log n)
             fib-style multi self-call (- n 1)/(- n 2) → O(2^n)
             rows inside map / nested maps → O(n²)
             no recursion/loops/DB list forms → O(1)
           Free badge on /binding next to effects. Optional later:
           empirical curve-fit via (bench 'fn sizes) + step counts.

         ==========================================================
         PHASE 5 — LLM ANALYZE ON DEMAND
         ==========================================================
           (analyze 'fn) prim + POST /api/analyze/:name (private).
           Reuse llm_client; prefer per-user key (auth.user-llm-keys)
           then env LLM_API_KEY. Structured JSON:
             {complexity, confidence, rationale, notes}
           Cache data_rows function_analysis by sha1(pp_expr).
           htmx button on /binding swaps #analyze-result.
           Always label "LLM estimate (model, when)". Cross-check
           against function_stat steps when present. No auto-call
           on page load.

         ==========================================================
         PHASE 6 — INSIDE-OUT EXPLAINER
         ==========================================================
           Explain.peel : expr -> layer list (innermost first by
           max depth; siblings left-to-right). Annotate with prim
           name/effects, self-ref, tail, complexity when known.
           (explain 'fn) returns layers as readable structure.
           UI: collapsible explain-layer blocks on /binding.
           Optional LLM prose gloss per layer, cache by sexp hash;
           opt-in only (cost scales with depth).

         ==========================================================
         SHIP ORDER
         ==========================================================
           1, 2, 4 independent (can parallelize).
           3 after 2. 5 after 2 (+ benefits from 4).
           6 after 1+4; LLM gloss optional after 5.

         ==========================================================
         NON-GOALS
         ==========================================================
           - Claiming undecidable static big-O as ground truth
           - EXPLAIN ANALYZE on every DB prim call
           - Auto LLM spend on every /binding view
           - Full coverage-guided test selection (see image.inspector
             impact queue; related but separate)
           - Custom VM / full CPS just for profiling

         SUCCESS: a human or agent opening /binding/foo sees structure
         (recursion/tail), free cost guess (big-O + DB tier), measured
         last run when available, and can click for LLM analysis or
         peel without leaving the browser.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection lazy-env-thunks
   (doc "VThunk: memoized lazy bindings; env LOAD stores recipes, first reference cooks them; explicit (delay)/(force) surface")
   (status implemented)
   (implements
    lisp/lib/ast.ml
    lisp/lib/env.ml
    lisp/lib/eval.ml
    lisp/test/lazy_thunk_test.ml)
   (verify
    (build "dune build lisp")
    (test "dune runtest lisp: thunk forces once, memoizes value AND failure, cyclic force raises, delay/force round-trip")
    (smoke "(force (delay (+ 1 2))) = 3; forcing twice runs body once"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         MOTIVATION (measured on prod 2026-08-21)
           Session.load_defs re-EVALUATED every function_defs body on
           every rehydrate: ~4013 defs on admin/personal took 60-120s
           under the global request mutex whenever HTTP identity flipped
           (one shared session, anonymous public traffic interleaving
           with authed agent traffic). But ~98% of defs are lambdas
           nobody touches between rebuilds: pure define is ~1us; the
           cost was STRICTNESS AT THE WRONG BOUNDARY, not interpreter
           speed and not per-def Postgres round trips (the load query
           is one recursive CTE). Habitat note spec/lazy-thunks
           (2026-08-20) already argued the general shape: the value is
           in MEMOIZATION at an EXPLICIT boundary, not pervasive
           call-by-need.

         KERNEL
           New value constructor in lisp/lib/ast.ml:

             VThunk of thunk_cell ref
             thunk_cell = Unforced of thunk_payload
                        | Forcing
                        | Forced of value
                        | Failed of exn
             thunk_payload = { body : expr;
                               def_env : env;
                               force : env -> expr -> value }

           The payload CARRIES ITS OWN EVALUATOR so the pure kernel
           stays I/O-free while the image can force under its prim
           rim (DB prims inside a forced body work) and under the
           current eval budget. Forcing MEMOIZES on all outcomes:
           Forced replays the value; Failed replays the exception
           (a def that errors at first touch does not re-tax every
           later touch); Forcing detects self-reference cycles and
           raises Interp_error instead of diverging.

         SINGLE OBSERVATION POINT
           Env.lookup is the one auto-force site. A VThunk never
           escapes into user values through a Var reference; the
           language stays strict everywhere else. Direct OCaml
           lookups (route handler resolution, ui shell) get the same
           semantics for free. Existing code never sees VThunk except
           via fold/list-bindings (renders <thunk>).

         USER SURFACE
           (delay EXPR)  -> VThunk; EXPR not evaluated
           (force X)     -> forces X if a thunk, else returns X
           (define ...) stays EAGER: laziness is a property of env
           loading and explicit delay, never of define. Side-effect
           order of ordinary code is unchanged.

         NON-GOALS
           - call-by-need language semantics (rejected in
             spec/lazy-thunks: side-effect order + budget attribution)
           - transparent forcing at truthiness/printing observation
             points (only Var forces; decide more after dogfooding)
           - cross-session or persistent thunk cache (Design B of the
             note — tree pending-state — remains a separate item)
    |))
   )
  )
 )
)
